<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-science/foundational/ecosystems-energy">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    An ecosystem is an energy pipeline with terrible efficiency. Sunlight hits producers —
    plants, algae — which convert roughly 1–2% of it into biomass. That energy then flows upward
    through <strong>trophic levels</strong> (herbivores, small predators, big predators), and at
    every transfer about <strong>90% is lost</strong> — spent on movement, warmth, and
    indigestible parts, or passed to decomposers. The "ten-percent rule" (Raymond Lindeman,
    1942) is the rule of thumb that falls out.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="why there are no wolf-eating dragons — energy at each level, on the ten-percent rule" widget="plotter" config={tenPctCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the curve honestly: by the fourth consumer level, a thousand units of plant energy have
    dwindled to 0.1. That is why food chains rarely run past four or five links, why big
    predators need huge territories, and why apex carnivores are always the first to vanish when
    habitat shrinks.
  </p>

  <h2>Consequences worth carrying</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pyramids everywhere.</strong> Biomass and numbers pile up the same way: a meadow supports millions of insects, dozens of birds, one kestrel.</li>
    <li><strong>Eating lower helps more.</strong> A vegetarian diet is, energetically, a shorter pipeline — the arithmetic behind the land-use arguments for plant-rich diets.</li>
    <li><strong>Decomposers close the loop.</strong> Fungi and bacteria return every molecule to the soil — without them, nutrients would lock away in corpses and the cycle would jam.</li>
    <li><strong>Keystone species punch above their energy budget.</strong> Remove wolves from Yellowstone (reintroduced 1995) or otters from kelp forests and whole landscapes reorganise. Ecosystems are networks, not just pipelines.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Lindeman, Ecology (1942); Odum, Fundamentals of Ecology; US National Park Service (Yellowstone wolf reintroduction).</p>
</TopicPage>
